{
  "dom_id": "step-q14081",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Solve $12{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2}-6y-5=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2}-6y-5 & = \\\\ (12{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2})-(6y+5) & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}(6y+5)-(6y+5) & = \\\\ (6y+5)\\cdot 2{y}^{2}-(6y+5)\\cdot 1 & = \\\\ (6y+5)(2{y}^{2}-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(6y+5)(2{y}^{2}-1)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(6y+5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+5 & =0 \\\\ 6y & =-5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.$",
